                "id": "conclusion-section-p22",
                "text": "It is very important to distinguish between the epochal definitions of today’s conservative from that of the concept of conservative. Apple’s argument is that today’s conservatives align with the preservationist nature of conceptual conservatism. In later sections, I discuss how it is the burden of progressives to force upon change. Neither term is meant to relate to the progressive party of politics nor the conservative ideals of republicans. It is true that the conservative party in the United States does not have direct control over education at points in history; yet, the ideals of preserving the nature of education would, syllogistically, be the dominant group. Dominance fears change – change threatens the parameters of such dominance. Progressives, in turn, are the subordinate who crave the change necessary to equal the playing field or become the dominant.  ↑",
